  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This study analyzed the spatiotemporal patterns and drivers of grassland net ecosystem productivity (NEP) across China using long-term remote sensing, environmental, and management datasets processed through an XGBoost-SHAP model. The model revealed that NEP increased across 73.44% of China's grasslands, particularly in the northeast and the eastern Qinghai-Tibet Plateau, with grazing intensity emerging as the most critical management predictor. Grazing intensity exhibited a highly nonlinear relationship with NEP and interacted strongly with local environmental factors like elevation, temperature, vapor pressure deficit, and slope.",
      "why": "It quantifies the nonlinear and highly localized impacts of grazing and management practices on carbon sequestration at a national scale, replacing broad assumptions with region-specific evidence of how environmental conditions alter management outcomes."
    }
  },
